  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 1225 AM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nForecast trends are on an upward trajectory for rain/storm \nchances Sunday-Sunday night, as the next in our series of systems \npasses over the region. Ridging in between these systems will give\nway to southwest flow, allowing for quality 700mb theta-e to be \nadvected back to the Panhandles. The window still appears somewhat\nbrief, but vigorous lift ahead of and with the trough's arrival \nshould help generate showers and storms for the Panhandles as \nearly as Sunday afternoon, but more likely Sunday evening- \novernight (40-70% POPs). Shear is forecast to be quite nice during\nthis time frame (40-60 kts), but instability is still looking \ndubious at best (70-90% chance for MUCAPE to stay <1000 J/kg). \nThis should limit severe potential, but a few organized strong \nstorms certainly can't be ruled out. A dry slot is slated to \nfilter in by Monday morning, bringing a brief end to precipitation\nchances. Temperatures to start the week Sun & Mon will be near \nseasonal averages in the 60s & 70s. Breezy south winds of 15-30 \nmph will become more common especially Monday, with gusts \napproaching 45 mph. \n\nAs the trough axis translates east of the Panhandles, a stout 120kt \nupper level jet streak within northwest flow will dive over the \nregion, allowing a strong cold front to push southward Monday night. \nThis front will have a tightly packed pressure gradient behind it, \nalong with decently stacked jet dynamics. While this supports cooler \ntemperatures being advected to the Panhandles, the more impactful \nconsequence appears to be the return of strong wind gusts. Despite \nthe potential for additional wrap around showers and dense cloud \ncover behind the front, this downward momentum could help gusts as \nhigh as 50-60 mph mix down to the surface if everything comes \ntogether just right (favoring the northwest Panhandles). Even if \nlimiting factors win out, it is likely that northwest winds of 20-30 \nmph gusting up to 45 mph will continue through much of the day \nTuesday. Rain chances during this time frame look to favor the \nnorthern combined Panhandles for now. \n\nModels are in fair agreement that our pattern should quiet down \nthrough the middle of next week, with weak ridging atop the region. \nTemps should be near or below seasonal averages, with drier air back \nin place for a couple days. Winds will thankfully diminish as well, \nspelling a recipe for some nice fall days. Long range projections \nare back to hinting at another active stretch towards the end of \nnext week, as our rambunctious run of fall weather doesn't look to \nstop anytime soon.\n\nHarrel",
